  "id": "DrugBank_DB13762 - MONDO_2177",
  "verbalization": "Dexrabeprazole is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hyperinsulinism (disease) through a chain of indications that converge on gastrin-related abnormalities. The drug is linked to Zollinger-ellison Syndrome, a condition characterized by excessive gastrin production, which leads to increased gastric acid secretion. This gastrin excess is further associated with gastrin secretion abnormalities, which in turn are indicated by Roxatidine Acetate, a drug that is linked to Hyperinsulinism. Cimetidine, another drug associated with Dexrabeprazole, is also linked to Hyperinsulinism, suggesting a broader role in the disease pathway. The evidence suggests that Dexrabeprazole may influence gastrin secretion, potentially contributing to the development of Hyperinsulinism through mechanisms involving gastrointestinal dysfunction. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role of Dexrabeprazole in the pathophysiology of Hyperinsulinism, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
